2. How the 5% Threshold Is Measured

   (a) The "Five Percent Threshold" is met when the Used Portion is more
       than five percent (5%) of the Original Work by EITHER of these
       measures (whichever gives the higher percentage):
         (1) lines: non-blank lines of text; or
         (2) characters: characters other than spaces, tabs, and line
             breaks.

   (b) Both measures are taken on the Original Work's own files, exactly
       as the Licensor published them, NOT on your work. So it does not
       matter how big your work is, how you format it, or how you split,
       join, pad, minify, or rewrite lines in it.

   (c) The whole Original Work (the number you divide by) is the single
       published version of the Original Work you took material from. If
       you took material from more than one version, use the smallest of
       those versions. Versions are never added together. Third-party
       material, automatically generated files (such as lock files or build
       output), and duplicate copies of the same file are not counted in
       either the Used Portion or the whole.

   (d) Everything you and your Affiliates have ever made Commercial Use of
       is added together. You may not get under 5% by splitting material
       across several products, versions, releases, updates, services,
       companies, or people.

   (e) The Original Work itself, with or without changes, always meets the
       Five Percent Threshold.

   (f) If you make Commercial Use of a Covered Work, it is your job to prove
       that it does not meet the Five Percent Threshold. Within 30 days of
       a written request from the Licensor, you must provide enough of your
       work's source code and content (which may be kept confidential) to
       check this. If you do not, the threshold is treated as met.

